# Atlas — committed configuration. This file is the source of truth.
#
# Precedence, lowest to highest:
#   1. this file                  committed, holds everything non-secret
#   2. Doppler                    secrets only (*_API_KEY), when a session exists
#   3. .env                       optional, gitignored, ABSENT by default
#
# The stack runs correctly with no .env at all — that is the normal state. Create
# one only to override a value on one machine, containing just the lines you are
# changing. Everything else keeps coming from here.
#
# Edit this file, then `make restart-build`. A plain `make restart` will NOT pick
# it up: container environment is fixed when the container is created.
# See docs/configuration.md and docs/superpowers/specs/2026-07-29-configuration-sources-design.md.
#
# Host paths mounted read-only into the stack (compose maps them to /data/*).
# Derived, not written down, so the same file works on any host, user or
# checkout path: ATLAS_REPO_PARENT is exported by the Makefile (this repo's
# parent directory — Atlas indexes the tree it lives in) and HOME comes from
# the shell. Compose interpolates `${...}` in env files. To index a different
# tree on one machine, override in .env — never with an absolute path here.
CODE_ROOT_HOST=${ATLAS_REPO_PARENT:?run compose through make — it exports ATLAS_REPO_PARENT}
CLAUDE_PROJECTS_HOST=${HOME:?HOME is not set}/.claude/projects

# Extra project trees to index (up to four). Uncomment and point at any folder
# holding more projects; each is mounted read-only. Apply with
# `make restart-build` after changing these — no reindex flag needed.
#CODE_ROOT_HOST_2=${HOME}/Work
#CODE_ROOT_HOST_3=/Volumes/CloudBox/Projects
#CODE_ROOT_HOST_4=
#CODE_ROOT_HOST_5=

# In-container paths (leave as-is when running under compose).
CODE_ROOT=/data/code
CLAUDE_PROJECTS_DIR=/data/claude/projects

# Infrastructure.
DATABASE_URL=postgres://kdbscope:kdbscope@postgres:5432/kdbscope
REDIS_URL=redis://redis:6379
QDRANT_URL=http://qdrant:6333
# Where Qdrant's data volume is mounted read-only into the API, so the dashboard
# can report real disk usage — Qdrant exposes no API for it.
QDRANT_STORAGE_PATH=/qdrant-storage

# Indexing.
SCAN_INTERVAL_MIN=5
# Parallel scan jobs. A local Ollama serialises embed requests, so more workers
# only deepen its queue. Raise for a remote/batched embedding endpoint.
WORKER_CONCURRENCY=2
# Kill switch for the sparse re-tokenisation pass (see docs/configuration.md).
# Leave true: a tokeniser change that is not applied to stored vectors makes
# keyword search silently stop matching. Set false only to stop a misbehaving
# rebuild; it retries on the next boot.
KDB_SPARSE_REBUILD=true
# Let EMBEDDINGS_PROVIDER=auto move the index to a new collection after falling
# back to a non-preferred embedder. Leave false: the fallback is a guess, and the
# consequence is a full re-embed plus the old collection being reclaimed.
KDB_ALLOW_EMBEDDER_DOWNGRADE=false

# Embeddings: auto | ollama | bundled | openai | g2p
# 'auto' prefers Ollama (much faster, 768-dim) and pulls the model on first
# boot; it falls back to the bundled CPU model with a warning if Ollama is down.
EMBEDDINGS_PROVIDER=auto
EMBEDDINGS_MODEL=nomic-embed-text
EMBEDDINGS_BASE_URL=
# Secret. Left empty here on purpose: a key belongs in Doppler, which overrides
# this file. Only needed for a keyed endpoint (provider=openai); Ollama and the
# local G2P proxy need none.
EMBEDDINGS_API_KEY=
OLLAMA_URL=http://host.docker.internal:11434

# Ask-mode LLM: openai | g2p   (g2p = OpenAI-compatible proxy, no key needed)
LLM_PROVIDER=g2p
LLM_MODEL=gemini-2.5-flash
LLM_BASE_URL=http://host.docker.internal:8181/v1
# Secret — see EMBEDDINGS_API_KEY above. G2P needs no key.
LLM_API_KEY=

# Doc staleness. Query-time ranking only: changing either never needs a reindex.
# Months since a doc's mtime past which it is labelled `aging` (no rank penalty —
# an old runbook that simply never needed edits must not be buried).
KDB_DOCS_AGING_MONTHS=12
# Score multiplier for docs under archive-style paths (0-1; lower buries deeper).
KDB_ARCHIVED_PENALTY=0.6
# Minimum token containment (0-1) linking a legacy DONE:/RESOLVED: backlog line to its item
KDB_BACKLOG_MATCH_THRESHOLD=0.5

# Usage monitoring (Monitor view / `atlas usage`). Every API request is recorded,
# polling included; the noise is separated at read time by route class, not by
# refusing to write it.
# Rows per page in the call log.
KDB_USAGE_PAGE_SIZE=100
# Minutes between adoption recomputes. Daily: the scan reads every Claude
# transcript on the machine, and whether agents honour a trigger moves over days.
KDB_ADOPTION_REFRESH_MIN=1440
# Days of telemetry to keep. 0 keeps everything, which is the default — at
# observed volume the whole table is single-digit MB/year, and silently
# discarding the record a monitor exists to hold is the worse failure. Pruning is
# a manual `make usage-prune DAYS=n`, never scheduled.
KDB_USAGE_RETENTION_DAYS=0

# Caller identity sent to G2P as `X-G2P-Client-Id` on every LLM *and* embedding
# call, so Atlas's token spend is attributed to us in G2P's /hstats instead of
# landing in the anonymous bucket. Telemetry only — it never affects routing —
# and harmless against non-G2P OpenAI-compatible endpoints, which ignore unknown
# X- headers. Unset uses the default below; set it per instance to tell two
# deployments apart; set it explicitly empty to send no header at all.
KDB_G2P_CLIENT_ID=Atlas

# Service ports (host side).
API_PORT=8710
MCP_PORT=8711
UI_PORT=8712
QDRANT_PORT=6363
QDRANT_GRPC_PORT=6364
REDIS_PORT=6390
POSTGRES_PORT=5460

# LAN exposure + auth (spec §7). api/mcp/ui bind here; qdrant/redis/postgres
# always stay 127.0.0.1 regardless. Leave at 127.0.0.1 for a single-user local
# install — legacy mode, no token required. Set to 0.0.0.0 to serve the LAN,
# but ONLY together with ATLAS_TOKEN below: api and mcp refuse to boot
# (fail closed) when this is non-loopback and no token is set, rather than
# serve unauthenticated. Threat model: trusted home LAN over cleartext HTTP,
# not a hostile network — see the spec for the documented TLS upgrade path.
# Caveat: as of this task, only the UI (its TokenGate prompt) and a raw curl
# with an explicit `Authorization: Bearer …` header can actually reach a
# LAN-bound instance — the `atlas connect`/CLI token clients that read
# ~/.atlas/credentials land in a later task of this feature.
ATLAS_BIND=127.0.0.1
# Secret. Left empty here on purpose: a key belongs in Doppler, which
# overrides this file (see EMBEDDINGS_API_KEY above). Required once
# ATLAS_BIND is not 127.0.0.1.
ATLAS_TOKEN=

# Internal URL the mcp/cli/ui use to reach the API.
KDBSCOPE_API_URL=http://api:8710

# Public access: Cloudflare tunnel + Cloudflare Access (docs/public-access.md).
#
# Atlas runs its OWN tunnel container (the `cloudflared` service), which dials
# the `ui` service from inside this compose network. Nothing extra is published
# on the host, and no other project's tunnel or nginx is involved — so a mistake
# here cannot take an unrelated site down.
#
# The tunnel service sits behind the `public` compose profile: with no hostname
# set it never starts, and Atlas stays exactly as local as it is today. Bring it
# up with `make public-up` once `make tunnel-setup` has provisioned Cloudflare.
#
# These two identify ONE deployment — a hostname you own and the people allowed
# through Access. THIS REPOSITORY IS PUBLIC, so they are empty slots here and
# belong in the gitignored .env, exactly like the API keys above.
ATLAS_PUBLIC_HOSTNAME=
ATLAS_ACCESS_EMAILS=
# Secret: the tunnel's own credential, printed by `make tunnel-setup`. Empty
# here for the same reason as EMBEDDINGS_API_KEY.
CLOUDFLARE_TUNNEL_TOKEN=
# Non-identifying defaults — no reason to change these per deployment.
ATLAS_TUNNEL_NAME=atlas
ATLAS_TUNNEL_ORIGIN=http://ui:80
# How long an Access browser session lasts before re-authenticating.
ATLAS_ACCESS_SESSION=720h
# Name of the Access service token the native app authenticates with.
ATLAS_ACCESS_TOKEN_NAME=atlas-native-app

# Multi-machine (spec: docs/superpowers/specs/2026-08-19-multi-machine-design.md).
# The fleet lives in config/machines.yaml (gitignored, per deployment — copy
# config/machines.example.yaml; absent = single-machine mode). ATLAS_SELF names this
# machine's entry and belongs in the per-machine .env, NOT here.
ATLAS_MACHINES_FILE=/config/machines.yaml
# Host directory holding the dedicated `atlas_sync` SSH keypair, bind-mounted
# read-only into the indexer at /keys (docker-compose.yml). Compose interpolates
# this straight into the volumes: list — it is never read by packages/core.
# `~/…` is NOT used here: it is only expanded by Compose in *bind-mount source*
# paths (verified empirically against this repo's `docker compose` — see
# task-15-report.md), not reliably across contexts, so `${HOME}` is the portable
# form and matches how the volumes: line itself defaults when this is unset.
ATLAS_KEYS_DIR=${HOME}/.atlas/keys
